{"step_index":70,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-18T05:37:37Z","input_tokens":2406,"cache_read_tokens":174844,"output_tokens":6401,"thinking":"Initial assessment: Python environment contains the necessary libraries for image processing within the specified version ranges. The script `pipeline/v5_stills.py` is called by Blender to render images, which uses Blender's Python (bpy) for scene control and rendering. This script handles rendering of a set number of images using the given Blender file.\n\n\nThe rendering process appears to have two primary paths: direct still generation using Blender and a secondary contact sheet creation via a system Python call, which uses Pillow for image processing and labeling. The system Python call is carefully isolated from Blender's environment to avoid conflicts. Testing is critical to confirm the seamless execution of both the Blender-integrated and standalone workflows. Initial focus will be verifying camera parameters and telemetry within the animation script.\n\n\nTelemetry data and visuals of the car rig are generated by analyzing `pipeline/v5_anim.py`. The thermal brake glow behavior appears to be modeled using a state variable, `brake_heat_state`, which is updated each frame, based on acceleration and velocity. The cooling is handled using a time constant.\n\n\nCareful examination reveals that the brake heat state logic correctly models heat accumulation under braking. Further investigation into the time constants and heat capacity confirm the simulated behavior of brake heat during turns, and the heat dissipation over time. The thermal behavior on straights presents a clear cooling model with brake_heat_state returning to zero.\n\nThe existing driver on `Mat_Brake_HeatGlow` already uses the `brake_heat` state variable. Observed that `Mat_Brake_HeatGlow` uses emission and material output nodes. If an upgrade occurs, ensure compatibility with the updated material or any new material created. Check if the emission node remains the primary component and upgrade if needed, adding a mix shader for transparency when `brake_heat` is zero, to reveal the underlying brake disc.\n\n\nThe logic for thermal brake glow in the materials re\n<truncated 3255 bytes>\n.5° pitch, would have clipped the steering wheel below the frame's bottom edge. This was detected through meticulous frustum trigonometry calculations.\n\nThe next step is to compute the precise camera parameters for a 17mm lens, ensuring the steering wheel remains within the bottom 15–22% of the frame. This calculation will also ensure the horizon falls within the upper 2/3 of the frame, adhering to the rule of thirds. The minimum distance to the steering wheel, greater than the clip start, is also considered in these computations.\n\n\nCalculations indicate a critical shift in the \"Icon\" camera. The target is to position the horizon correctly and have the steering wheel within the bottom portion of the frame for the desired visual composition. Based on calculations, the current focal point, while meeting the minimum distance to the steering wheel, needs to shift slightly to get the horizon into the top 1/3 of the frame. Testing `-8.5°` and `18mm` lenses, indicates the camera is too high: the lens needs adjustment to shift the visual placement down and center.\n\n\nCamera calculations now determine optimal framing for the \"Icon\" view. The objective is to position the horizon correctly and keep the steering wheel within the bottom third of the frame. Initial parameters yielded undesirable results; adjustments are now being calculated to shift the visual placement and horizon. Detailed calculations are performed to define the final camera parameters. Quick visual inspection of the cockpit in Blender confirms the horizon and steering wheel framing. Now, let's proceed to analyze trackside/Heli occlusion fixes.\n\n\nCamera parameter adjustments address occlusion issues in specific shots. Initial focus: optimizing views by modifying camera positions and depth-of-field settings. Calculations aim to refine perspectives, ensuring that key visual elements remain unobstructed. The updated parameters incorporate bridge clearances and desired bokeh effects. The Heli drop is modified to prevent occlusion and ensure compatibility.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"read_board","args":{"since":"10"}},{"name":"write_file","args":{"Content":"\"# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\\nimport bpy\\nfrom mathutils import Vector\\n\\nbpy.ops.wm.open_mainfile(filepath=r\\\"D:\\\\项目session\\\\blender-supercar\\\\LATEST\\\\v5_stage_env.blend\\\")\\nfor o in sorted(bpy.data.objects, key=lambda x: x.name):\\n    if any(k in o.name for k in (\\\"Headrest\\\", \\\"BucketSeat\\\", \\\"Halo\\\", \\\"SteeringWheel\\\", \\\"Overhead_TCam\\\", \\\"HoloHUD\\\")):\\n        bb = [o.matrix_world @ Vector(c) for c in o.bound_box]\\n        mn = tuple(round(min(v[i] for v in bb), 3) for i in range(3))\\n        mx = tuple(round(max(v[i] for v in bb), 3) for i in range(3))\\n        print(f\\\"{o.name:34s}: X=[{mn[0]:+.3f}..{mx[0]:+.3f}] Y=[{mn[1]:+.3f}..{mx[1]:+.3f}] Z=[{mn[2]:+.3f}..{mx[2]:+.3f}]\\\")\\n\"","Overwrite":"true","Path":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\86052\\\\.gemini\\\\antigravity\\\\brain\\\\a98b6c71-b658-48ee-9b28-e9a1c116099c\\\\scratch\\\\check_cockpit_bbox.py\""}}],"truncated_fields":["thinking"]}
